   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general business law, in relation to the percentage of\nunits to be sold to convert certain real property to cooperative or\ncondominium ownership in the city of New York",
    "basePrintNo": "S8564",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8564-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 17,
       "fullName": "Simcha Felder",
       "imgName": "439_simcha_felder.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 439,
       "sessionMemberId": 1805,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "FELDER"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §352-eeee, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8564\n\nSPONSOR: GOUNARDES\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to the percentage\nof units to be sold to convert certain real property to cooperative or\ncondominium ownership in the city of New York\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo permit owner-occupied buildings with five or fewer units to convert\nto cooperative or condominium ownership with fewer restrictions.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of this bill amends section 352-eeee of the General Business\nLaw to provide that for buildings containing five or fewer units, a plan\nmay not be declared effective for conversion to cooperative or condomin-\nium ownership until written purchase agreements have been executed and\ndelivered from either bona fide tenants in occupancy or bona fide\npurchasers for occupancy for at least fifteen percent of all dwelling\nunits in the building.\n\nSection two makes conforming amendments to subdivision two of section\n352-eeee of the General Business Law.\n\nSection three sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nRecent changes to Section 352-eeee of the General Business Law as\namended by Part N of Chapter 36 of 2019 have severely impacted communi-\nties with small buildings occupied by homeowners. There are many small\nbuildings in New York City with a limited number of apartments that are\noccupied by the owner and several tenants. Until the recent change, only\n15% of the units had to be sold to effectively convert the building to a\ncondominium. Due to the change in the law, if 51% of the tenants now do\nnot buy their respective units, the building can no longer be converted\nto a condominium and the owners cannot sell their unit. This has a nega-\ntive impact on both tenants and owners of units in these small build-\nings.\n\nOn the tenant side, it serves as an additional obstacle to homeowner-\nship. Before the change in the law, a tenant occupying a unit in a three\nto five family house had the option to negotiate the purchase of the\nunit they had been renting and living in. This was an attractive option\nto tenants, and still is albeit the current obstacles. It is a home they\nhave been living in and are comfortable with and they are aware of any\npotential issues with the unit. It is also a more affordable way to\nacquire a home relative to purchasing a single standing house, which is\nfrequently prohibitively expensive in New York City. This, in turn,\nwould also incentivize current owners to maintain and improve such rent-\ned units in the event that renters may become potential purchasers.\n\nOn the owner side, the change in law affects mobility. Owners occupying\nonly one unit of a three-family house and who are looking to relocate,\ncannot sell their specific unit, which could provide the funds necessary\nto purchase a new home.\nAs a result of the amendment, the ability to convert to condominium or\ncooperative ownership has become unrealistic for these small buildings.\nTo mitigate these concerns for both tenants and owners of these small\nbuildings, the law should be amended to allow buildings with five or\nfewer units to convert to cooperative or condominium ownership by sell-\ning fifteen percent of the units instead of the current fifty-one\npercent, as was allowed under the law before the amendment.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTBD\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after which it shall\nhave become a law.\n",
